## 1. Objective and scope

Determine why nominally identical 3D-printed polymer tensile coupons show persistent differences in tensile strength by:

- Quantifying variation attributable to material, printer, build, coupon location, process conditions, specimen geometry, conditioning, and tensile testing.
- Estimating which measurable factors mediate those differences.
- Distinguishing systematic manufacturing effects from measurement error and irreducible coupon-to-coupon variability.
- Testing competing mechanisms, including:
  - feedstock condition or lot variability;
  - thermal-history and interlayer-bond differences;
  - extrusion-flow or dimensional variation;
  - build-plate position and orientation effects;
  - machine- or build-specific effects;
  - voids, defects, and internal architecture;
  - environmental conditioning;
  - grip, alignment, strain-rate, or instrument effects.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength calculated from maximum force and measured initial cross-sectional area.

Secondary outcomes: maximum force, elastic modulus, yield stress where applicable, strain at break, fracture location and mode, density/void fraction, and dimensional variability.

The study applies to one prespecified polymer, coupon standard, printing technology, slicer workflow, and nominal process recipe. Generalization to other materials or geometries requires confirmation.

## 2. Assumptions

- Coupons comply with a prespecified tensile-test standard; the geometry, conditioning, test speed, and reporting rules are fixed before data collection.
- “Nominally identical” means identical digital model and nominal recipe, not necessarily identical realized geometry, thermal history, or microstructure.
- Production and testing processes remain sufficiently stable to be characterized, and all deviations, maintenance, failures, and operator interventions are logged.
- Each coupon can be traced to feedstock lot and handling history, printer, nozzle, build, plate position, print sequence, and test session.
- Printers and test systems can be calibrated or independently verified before the study.
- Destructive tensile outcomes are compared with measurements taken before testing or with matched witness specimens.
- Candidate factors selected for manipulation are safe and lie within validated operating ranges.
- Specimens are the observational units, but builds—not individual coupons within a build—are the principal independent manufacturing replicates.
- Any strength difference deemed practically important is defined in advance, such as a minimum percentage change or engineering tolerance.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Process audit and measurement-system qualification

1. Freeze and archive:
   - CAD and mesh files;
   - slicer version, profile, and generated machine code;
   - printer firmware and relevant machine settings;
   - tensile-test method and data-processing code or worksheet.

2. Map the full process:
   - feedstock receipt, storage, drying, and loading;
   - printing, removal, labeling, conditioning, measurement, and testing;
   - handling by operators and any rework or exclusions.

3. Verify measurement systems using traceable or suitable reference artifacts:
   - tensile load, displacement, extensometer, alignment, and test speed;
   - dimensional instruments;
   - mass and environmental sensors;
   - printer nozzle, bed, and chamber temperature sensors;
   - filament diameter or resin-delivery measurements, as applicable.

4. Conduct a crossed repeatability-and-reproducibility study:
   - multiple operators repeatedly measure a blinded specimen set;
   - include re-fixturing for tensile-machine alignment and grip procedures where feasible;
   - estimate instrument, operator, and specimen contributions to dimensional and nondestructive measurements.

   Destructive tensile repeatability should be assessed with randomized matched coupons, not repeated tests of the same specimen.

### Phase B: Baseline variance-components study

Produce coupons across multiple independent builds, days, feedstock lots, printers, and operators. Include several coupons at distributed, prespecified plate locations in each build.

Key controls:

- Randomize build order and testing order.
- Rotate or randomize coupon position and orientation across builds using a balanced layout.
- Block factors that cannot be randomized conveniently, including printer, day, or lot.
- Blind tensile-test personnel to manufacturing condition where practical.
- Condition all specimens under controlled temperature and humidity for a fixed interval.
- Use standardized handling, grip pressure, alignment, preload, strain measurement, and exclusion criteria.
- Include periodic reference coupons or a stable reference material during tensile sessions to detect tester drift.
- Preserve repeated production runs over time rather than relying on one large build.

This phase estimates variation at the lot, printer, build, plate-region, test-session, and coupon levels while recording continuous process covariates.

### Phase C: Randomized mechanism-testing experiment

Select factors based on the baseline audit, but define a candidate list in advance. Use a blocked factorial or response-surface design rather than changing one factor at a time. Candidate controlled factors may include:

- feedstock moisture or drying condition;
- nozzle or process temperature;
- bed and chamber temperature;
- cooling intensity;
- print speed and volumetric flow;
- layer height and extrusion-width ratio;
- raster orientation and toolpath sequence;
- coupon plate position and orientation;
- nozzle wear state;
- printer identity;
- conditioning humidity and duration;
- tensile-test alignment, grip protocol, and test session.

Separate manufacturing hypotheses from testing hypotheses:

- To test manufacturing effects, randomly assign manufacturing conditions within balanced printer/day/lot blocks and test specimens in randomized, blinded order.
- To test testing-system effects, distribute matched coupons from the same builds across test machines, operators, sessions, grip setups, or laboratories if available.
- Where practical, split coupons from each build across test sessions so build and test effects are not confounded.
- Include center-point or replicated nominal-condition builds to detect drift, nonlinearity, and pure error.
- Replicate each condition in independent builds; multiple coupons per build characterize within-build variability but do not substitute for build replication.

### Phase D: Targeted confirmation

Repeat the nominal condition and the most explanatory intervention on new feedstock and new production dates. Confirm that:

- the hypothesized factor changes the proposed intermediate measurement;
- the intermediate measurement predicts strength within condition;
- controlling or correcting the factor reduces the original persistent difference;
- the result reproduces across printers or lots within the intended scope.

### Discriminating observations

| Competing explanation | Observation supporting it | Observation arguing against it |
|---|---|---|
| Feedstock moisture or lot | Strength tracks measured moisture or lot; controlled drying reduces differences and changes void/fracture characteristics | Differences persist after equilibrated conditioning and across randomized lots |
| Thermal/interlayer bonding | Strength tracks recorded thermal history, chamber gradients, weld morphology, or bonding-sensitive orientation; temperature intervention shifts both microstructure and strength | No thermal gradient or bonding change accompanies strength differences |
| Flow or geometry | Maximum force and measured area change systematically with flow or location; area correction alters the strength pattern; mass-per-length or wall dimensions predict outcomes | Strength differences remain with precise local geometry and nearly constant force/area metrics |
| Internal voids or defects | Nondestructive imaging or density predicts strength and fracture origin; settings that reduce voids improve strength | Void metrics are stable and unrelated to strength or fracture |
| Build-plate position | A reproducible spatial gradient appears across rotated layouts or repeated builds and follows machine coordinates rather than specimen labels | Position effects disappear or do not replicate |
| Printer or hardware | Differences follow printer/nozzle after identical material, build design, and tester assignment; maintenance or component swap changes outcomes | Differences follow material or test session rather than hardware |
| Conditioning | Mass, moisture, or strength changes with controlled conditioning, and group differences converge after equilibrium | Equilibrated coupons retain the same differences |
| Tensile testing | Results follow tester, operator, alignment, grip, or session; matched coupons measured elsewhere disagree; reference checks drift | Blinded randomized matched coupons agree across testing conditions |
| Random specimen variability | No stable group or covariate effect replicates, while residual variation remains exchangeable | Persistent effects reproduce and respond to intervention |

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Traceability and metadata

For every coupon:

- unique coupon, build, printer, operator, lot, spool or resin-batch, nozzle, and test-session identifiers;
- source file and checksum or version;
- all nominal slicer and machine settings;
- build start time, sequence, plate coordinates, orientation, and neighboring parts;
- maintenance, calibration, interruptions, alarms, and handling deviations;
- time and conditions between printing, removal, conditioning, measurement, and testing.

### Material evidence

- manufacturer lot and relevant certification data;
- filament diameter and ovality, or equivalent resin-delivery characteristics;
- feedstock and coupon mass before and after drying or conditioning;
- direct moisture measurement where available;
- storage temperature, humidity, and duration;
- indicators of degradation or contamination, if applicable.

### Manufacturing evidence

- actual nozzle, bed, chamber, and ambient temperature time series;
- humidity and airflow;
- actual versus commanded speed, flow, and extrusion information where measurable;
- energy, pressure, torque, or motor-load proxies if available;
- nozzle wear and dimensional checks;
- build-surface flatness and leveling;
- process interruptions and layer times.

### Coupon evidence

- width and thickness at multiple gauge locations;
- gauge length, flatness, warpage, surface roughness, and mass;
- density or volume estimate;
- nondestructive defect/void measurements on a prespecified subset or all coupons if feasible;
- photographs before and after testing;
- fracture location and classified fracture mode;
- microstructural or fractographic observations from a stratified sample of high-, middle-, and low-strength coupons.

### Tensile-test evidence

- raw force, crosshead displacement, and extensometer strain time series;
- actual test speed, preload, grip type and pressure, alignment metrics, machine, operator, and session;
- calibration and reference-check records;
- invalid-test indicators such as grip failure, slippage, or fracture outside the valid region.

A prospective sample-size simulation should use pilot variance components and the minimum important effect. It must account for clustering within builds, multiple candidate factors, plausible interactions, and planned confirmation. If no pilot information exists, begin with a balanced pilot designed primarily to estimate variance components, then lock the confirmatory sample size without testing final hypotheses on the same data.

## 5. Measurement

- Measure width and thickness at prespecified gauge locations with validated instruments and reported resolution. Define whether strength uses mean local area, minimum area, or the applicable standard’s rule before testing.
- Retain maximum force separately from calculated strength to distinguish load-bearing changes from area-calculation effects.
- Prefer direct strain measurement with a calibrated extensometer or digital image method; do not rely solely on crosshead displacement for modulus or strain at break.
- Synchronize clocks across printers, environmental sensors, and testing systems.
- Calibrate or verify sensors at the beginning and end of the campaign and at fixed intervals.
- Use automated data capture where possible and prohibit undocumented manual transcription.
- Code fracture location and mode using a blinded rubric; assess agreement between independent raters on a subset.
- Perform nondestructive imaging, density measurement, or surface metrology before tensile testing. Define image-processing and defect thresholds before examining strength associations.
- Analyze microscopy or fractography with blinded specimen identifiers and balanced selection across experimental groups.
- Establish validity rules in advance:
  - instrument fault;
  - grip slippage;
  - fracture outside the permitted region;
  - dimensional nonconformance;
  - interrupted build;
  - missing critical metadata.

  Report invalid tests and exclusions by condition. Do not discard statistically extreme results solely because they are extreme.

## 6. Analysis

### Primary model

Fit a hierarchical mixed-effects model for tensile strength, with fixed effects for prespecified manipulated factors and relevant interactions, and random effects as appropriate for:

- feedstock lot or spool;
- printer;
- production day;
- build;
- plate region or spatial surface;
- tensile machine, operator, and session;
- coupon-level residual variation.

Account for nesting and crossing explicitly. Model plate coordinates continuously or spatially where justified rather than relying only on coarse location labels.

Report:

- estimated effects with confidence intervals;
- variance components and intraclass correlations;
- marginal and conditional model fit;
- prediction error under build- or day-held-out cross-validation;
- absolute effects and percent effects relative to the nominal mean;
- probability or interval relative to the prespecified practical threshold.

### Measurement-error adjustment

Use measurement-system estimates to separate observed variance from gauge variability. Propagate uncertainty in cross-sectional area into strength estimates. Compare models for maximum force, cross-sectional area, and tensile strength to identify artifacts caused by normalization.

### Mechanism analysis

For each proposed mechanism, specify:

1. the intervention or exogenous contrast;
2. the intermediate measurement, such as moisture, thermal history, void fraction, gauge area, or alignment;
3. the predicted direction of change;
4. the tensile response;
5. the expected fracture signature.

Evaluate whether intervention effects on strength are accompanied by predicted changes in the intermediate measurement. Mediation estimates may be reported as mechanistic evidence, but causal mediation claims require explicit assumptions and sensitivity analysis for unmeasured mediator–outcome confounding.

### Alternative explanations and diagnostics

- Test time trends, warm-up effects, nonlinear responses, heteroscedasticity, and influential builds.
- Examine whether residual variance differs by printer, lot, or condition.
- Check confounding and overlap before interpreting observational baseline associations.
- Correct or hierarchically model multiplicity for secondary factors and interactions.
- Use prespecified transformations or robust models for nonnormal outcomes; do not select transformations solely to improve significance.
- Analyze missingness and invalid tests by condition and perform sensitivity analyses under plausible missing-data mechanisms.
- Compare conclusions with and without protocol deviations, but retain intention-to-produce analyses as the primary operational result.
- Keep exploratory findings distinct from confirmatory results and require new-build replication.

### Evidence hierarchy

Interpret evidence in descending order:

1. replicated randomized intervention with predicted intermediate and fracture changes;
2. randomized tester or machine reassignment;
3. reproducible variance component or spatial pattern;
4. prospective observational association with plausible temporal order;
5. post hoc correlation or isolated microscopy observation.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Before data collection, define:

- the minimum practically important strength difference;
- acceptable repeatability and reproducibility limits;
- the maximum residual between-build and within-build variation;
- primary hypotheses, contrasts, interactions, and family-wise error or false-discovery control;
- required precision for key effect and variance-component estimates.

Stop the baseline phase when confidence intervals for the principal variance components are narrow enough to distinguish whether each exceeds the predefined practical variance threshold, subject to minimum numbers of independent builds, days, printers, and lots.

Advance a factor to confirmation only if:

- its effect is practically important or materially reduces unexplained variance;
- uncertainty excludes effects too small to matter, or the evidence justifies further testing;
- direction and mechanism are coherent;
- the result is not driven by one build, printer, lot, or test session.

Conclude that a factor accounts for persistent differences only if a prespecified confirmatory intervention on new builds:

- reproduces the strength effect;
- changes the predicted intermediate measure;
- reduces the original group or batch difference or residual variance;
- passes robustness and validity checks.

Conclude that the testing system is the main source if matched manufacturing specimens yield systematic results by tester, operator, alignment, or session and manufacturing assignments do not explain the contrast.

Conclude that no investigated factor adequately explains the differences if all practically important effects are excluded with prespecified precision while residual variance remains. Report that result rather than continuing until significance appears.

Permit early stopping only for safety, equipment failure, gross protocol invalidity, or a prespecified overwhelming effect boundary. Do not stop because an interim result merely reaches conventional statistical significance.

## 8. Limitations

- Results may be specific to the selected polymer, geometry, print orientation, machines, slicer, environmental range, and tensile standard.
- Some process variables may remain correlated or poorly measured, limiting causal attribution in the baseline phase.
- Sensor readings may not represent temperatures, pressure, or moisture at the actual bond interface.
- Destructive testing prevents repeated tensile measurement of the same coupon; matched specimens are not perfectly identical.
- Nondestructive imaging and microscopy may have resolution limits or sample-selection bias.
- Feedstock aging, printer wear, software updates, and maintenance can introduce time-varying effects.
- Factor interactions may require more independent builds than operationally practical.
- Conditioning can alter specimens while reducing environmental variability, so findings may depend on the chosen conditioning protocol.
- Standard tensile outcomes may not predict fatigue, impact, creep, or performance of full-scale printed parts.
- Failure to detect an effect means it is below the study’s detectable or practical threshold under the investigated conditions, not that the mechanism is universally absent.

